ID: B2-Ch08a-Intro
TEXT:

"There you are!" Melindra calls out the moment we draw within earshot. Her silk clothes ruffle in the morning draft, hair tied up in that high ponytail. "True to the hour. Tell me, are we all ready for an adventure?"

"If by adventure you mean rotting in line for an hour, certainly," Daren says, nodding toward Thilias's northern gate.

Dozens of wagons choke the thoroughfare. Most belong to merchants hauling crates, barrels, and sacks of grain, each cart stalled before a checkpoint where city guards probe canvas covers and check manifest papers one crate at a time. Given the glacial pace of the inspections, Daren's estimate sounds downright charitable. We could easily sit here till noon.

"Right," Melindra says, scanning the snarl of oxen and wooden wheels. "I forgot the northern gate belongs to the trade caravans. You would think they could carve a second archway through the stone to unclog the traffic."

"More gates make more breaches to defend," Daren says. "Monster incursions are running rampant across the provinces. A single bottleneck keeps the city safe. We wait our turn like everyone else. The sun barely cleared the ridge—we have daylight to burn."

"I have a faster route," Melindra says, a grin spreading across her face. "A shortcut."

"A shortcut?" Hadrik scratches his thick black beard. "Through an underground drainage tunnel, or..."

"Nothing so filthy," Melindra says.

She snaps her fingers.

The ground drops away beneath our boots. Dense updrafts seize us by the heels and shoulders, lifting the entire group into the open air. The pressure coils around my limbs with the same buffeting hum I felt when I used the stat device's air magic against the stillwater skeletons—only here the lift is steady, effortless, and climbing fast.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Intro2
TEXT:

"No," Daren says, his boots dangling over the treetops as we climb toward the battlements. "Tell me you are not doing what I think you are doing."

"Doing what?" Melindra asks, floating upright with a coy tilt of her head.

"Do not play innocent with me!" Daren grips his scabbard. "You are hauling us straight over the city wall. Have you lost your mind? It is against municipal law! The garrison stationed on the parapet will open fire the second we clear the ramparts!"

"So what?" Melindra says. "You wear enchanted plate and carry a magical heater shield. Throw a ward over us and let them bounce off."

"I do not have a barrier designed for mundane arrows!" Daren yells back.

Melindra raises an eyebrow. "Seriously?"

"That is beside the point!" Daren snaps. "We just cleared our names in this city! I refuse to make an enemy of the entire kingdom because we lacked the patience to stand in line!"

"Why do you care?" Melindra asks. "Were you planning on coming back?"

"Yes! That is—I mean—possibly! We might!"

"Too late to turn around," Melindra says, gesturing below. "Look down. We are directly over the stone. Archers are knocking their bows. I hope someone in this party knows how to block a volley, because keeping six people aloft demands real focus, and I would hate to drop one of you while batting away iron tips."

"Attention, unauthorized mages above the wall!" a voice booms through an acoustic brass magnifier from the ramparts. "Descend at once! I repeat: descend immediately! Disregard this order and you will be declared hostile combatants and fired upon! There will be no second warning!"

choice("Try to convince Melindra to bring us down.", B2-Ch08a-Tickle, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Tickle, v_b2_ch8_tickle = 1)
choice("Let Melindra deal with the situation however she wants.", B2-Ch08a-Situation, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Situation, v_b2_ch8_tickle = 0)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Tickle
TEXT:

"Melindra, you heard the man," I say. "Take us down so we can talk to him. We can still talk our way out of this before blood spills."

"And sit in a holding cell until sunset while they check our papers?" Melindra says. "No thank you. I prefer dodging arrows."

"It is not about the arrows!" I say. "We spent days getting pardoned by the king. I am not becoming a wanted fugitive again over five minutes of convenience!"

"Tough luck," Melindra says. "I set the route, and I do not stop for guards. If you think you can force a landing, be my guest. Let us see what an ordinary mortal tries against a stillwater of my—AHAHAHA—stop! Stop it or I will—ahahahaha!"

I step forward through the air currents, reach around both sides of her ribs, and dig my fingers into her waist.

Melindra shrieks, writhing midair. The updraft beneath us immediately stutters. The air thins, drops two feet, and surges back up in violent, sickening lurches as all six of us wobble like suspended puppets.

"Damn it!" Melindra gasps through breathless laughter. "I cannot—ahaha—I cannot twist away while holding six people in the sky! Stop... tickling me!"

On the battlements below, the guard captain lowers his arm. Bowstrings snap in unison. A dark swarm of iron-tipped shafts arcs upward straight for our bellies.

Kate acts instantly. Frost cracks across the air, congealing into four curved plates of thick ice that orbit our perimeter, deflecting the incoming volley with sharp, splintering clatters.

"Barry, what in the gods' names are you doing?" Daren shouts, clutching his scabbard as his legs swing wildly over empty space. "Do you want to snap our necks?"

"Relax," I call over the howling draft. "She is not going to drop us."

"Yes I will!" Melindra wheezes. "I will let go right now! Just you—ahahaha! Quit it!"

"Right," I say, keeping my fingers pressed into her ribs. "You searched a whole city for mercenaries to kill your king, and now you plan to splat us across the cobblestones? Put us down. The captain is waiting, and we can still talk."

"I will remember this!" Melindra gasps, her face flushed red. As soon as I pull my hands back, she steers the wind downward, lowering us toward the hard turf outside the moat.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Tickle2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Tickle2)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Tickle2
TEXT:

The guard captain gestures for his archers to hold their fire the moment our descent begins. He scrambles down an iron ladder fixed to the outer stone and strides across the ditch to meet us. He is a dwarf of Hadrik's broad proportions, clad in interlocking steel plates, a thick brown beard spilling over his gorget beneath an iron helm that leaves only his dark eyes and nose visible.

Daren and I meet him halfway, talking over each other to apologize and swear up and down that we meant no insult or siege.

"Ugh..." The captain closes his eyes, pressing thick gauntleted fingers against his brow. "If you needed an expedited exit, you could have spoken to the gate sergeant. Every man on that wall watched you brawl with the red dragon alongside King Golmyck yesterday. We would have waved you through the trade line in five seconds as a courtesy for your service. I will keep this off the garrison ledger, but by the gods, cease these stunts. We have enough trouble with monster hordes without tournament mages treating our ramparts like a hurdle."

"Understood," Daren says with a low bow. "You have our deepest gratitude, Captain. And our sincerest apologies. Next time we meet, I promise it will be under better circumstances."

We step back to rejoin the group. Melindra stands with her arms crossed and her lips pressed into a tight, furious pout, refusing to meet my gaze. Without a word, she pivots on her heel and marches into the brush, leaving us to fall in behind her.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Precautions, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Precautions)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Situation
TEXT:

"So you plan to simply ignore the garrison commander?" Daren asks Melindra as the wind bears us forward.

"Why not?" Melindra says, her eyes fixed on the horizon. "I doubt I will ever see his face again. Just keep the arrows off us until we clear their range. That is—not you! Someone with actual projectile wards. Someone handle the bowmen!"

The captain on the parapet barks an order, and the wall bristles with release. Dozens of arrows hiss through the air toward our formation.

Kate raises her left hand. Frost crystallizes in spinning discs around our flanks, forming thick ice shields that drift along our perimeter. The iron tips strike the frost and shatter or slide off into the ditch below.

"You know," Melindra calls back over her shoulder, "it would be considerably easier if you simply killed the archers."

"I will not murder guards for doing their duty," Kate says, her voice flat and frozen. "We committed the infraction, not them."

"Fine, fine," Melindra mutters. "I will push the draft. Hold on to your packs."

The air howls in our ears as our speed doubles. Within moments, the arrows fall short, dropping harmlessly into the grass behind us. The archers lower their bows, showing no appetite for a pursuit beyond the boundary stones.

"Splendid," Daren mutters, hanging in the slipstream. "I dread to think of the reception waiting for us if we ever have to enter Thilias again."

"You will be fine," Melindra says dismissively. "Tell the gatekeepers you need an audience with Golmyck and sort out the fines. He is not throwing dragon-slayers into a dungeon over a skipped turn in line. Right, this clearing will do. Brace for landing."

She sweeps both hands down, and the wind deposits us gently onto the tall grass beyond the tree line. Melindra straightens her silk cuffs and strides ahead without looking back.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Precautions, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Precautions)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Precautions
TEXT:

"Why are we leaving the road?" Kate asks as Melindra steers us into the dense timber. "The marker by the milestone pointed straight to Ollendor."

"The merchant road loops around the lowlands," Melindra says, ducking under a low-hanging bough. "Caravans take the long way through patrolled territory so they can dodge monster dens. We have no reason to waste two extra days on safety margins. Most of you are accomplished mages, after all. Nowhere near my league, obviously, but capable enough."

"Must you deliver an insult with every sentence?" I ask. "Your magic does not hold a candle to Eiden's. And as far as ordinary stillwaters go, our group already handled five of them at once. They were hardly invincible. I finished off the bulk of them myself."

"Cease your ridiculous bragging, ape!" Arraka's voice snaps from the amulet at Flower's neck. "If not for my tactical advice and that absurdly overpowered lessathi relic strapped to your wrist, those stillwaters would have ground your bones into paste!"

"Fine, we had leverage," I say. "Point is, that was a coordinated squad of five. Melindra is on her own, and not particularly frightening. From what she has displayed so far, she is an air elementalist. That is right, is it not?"

"What if I am?" Melindra asks, casting an annoyed glance back.

"Air is historically the weakest elemental discipline in the books," I say. "Even stacked against artificial variants the lessathi cooked up. Can you produce anything beyond a stiff breeze?"

"Of course I can!" Melindra's cheeks flush with anger.

"Splendid," I say. "Teleport the six of us straight to Ollendor's gates right now. Eiden would have done it without blinking."

"I am not Eiden," Melindra says.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Obliterated, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Obliterated)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Obliterated
TEXT:

"Fair enough," I say. "My mistake. But surely a legendary stillwater air mage could carry us the entire distance through the clouds? Oh, wait—I forgot you could barely stabilize six people while clearing a city wall. Flight is off the table, then? Interesting. To be honest, stillwaters are beginning to sound less like mythical calamities and more like regular casters with an oversized mana tank. Funny how that works."

Melindra halts, knitting her brows and pressing her lips into a hard, childish scowl.

"Barry, drop it," Daren warns, stepping between us. "Our journey just started. I will not have us bickering like stray dogs all the way to Ollendor."

"I am merely establishing facts," I say. "If she would admit she is not omnipotent and that our combined strength could take her down in a fight, we would all travel much easier."

"Would you like to test that theory?" Melindra smirks, cracking her knuckles with a series of sharp pops. "All we need is for your white mage to release the non-aggression clause in his oath, and we can settle this right here."

"Now you are speaking my language!" Hadrik says, grinning broadly as he rolls his shoulders.

"Works for me," I say.

"Ahahaha!" Arraka's cackle erupts from the gemstone as Kate drags a palm down her own face. "You will all be wiped out! That girl has zero concept of restraint! Ahahahaha!"

"Quiet!" Daren roars. "No one is fighting anyone! I am not suspending a sacred blood vow so you children can roughhouse in the brush! We are beyond the city wards! Hostile tournament competitors could strike at any second. We have no time for games!"

"Fine," Melindra mutters, her scowl deepening.

choice(""As you like."", B2-Ch08a-Whatever, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Whatever, v_b2_ch8_tornado = 0)
choice(""What if we only ask her to demonstrate her powers, without fighting us?"", B2-Ch08a-Tornado, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Tornado, v_b2_ch8_tornado = 1)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Tornado
TEXT:

"What if we only ask her to demonstrate her powers, without fighting us?"

Daren rubs his chin, considering. "I suppose it does not hurt to know our ally's range. Seeing as we have to coordinate in Ollendor..."

"Perfect!" Melindra's face brightens instantly. "Give me space. I need to get well clear of you, unless you want several tons of topsoil dumped on your heads."

"Wait," Daren says, holding up a hand. "What exactly do you intend to—"

She launches straight up before he can finish. The draft kicks leaves in our faces as she rockets into the upper sky, dwindling until her silhouette is little more than a speck against the clouds. High above the forest canopy, she halts and spreads her arms wide.

The air around us turns icy and thin. A low rumble builds in the distance, deepening into a deafening roar as the winds below her begin to spin. Within seconds, a colossal funnel of screaming air tears down from the sky, its vortex grinding into the earth half a mile away.

Uprooted oak trees spin through the cyclone like kindling. Massive boulders rip free from the hillside, tumbling through the roaring funnel while a blinding wall of dust and debris rolls outward across the plain. Far below on the merchant road, wagon drivers haul their oxen to a dead halt, gaping in terror at the monstrous twister tearing apart the horizon.

"All right, Melindra, point taken!" Daren shouts at the top of his lungs against the gale. "Bring it down! You are terrifying the countryside!"

The funnel hesitates, then unravels. The screaming wind subsides into a dying breeze over the next half-minute, dropping shattered trunks and pulverized rock in a wide perimeter across the wasteland. Melindra drifts back down through the settling dust, landing before us with a radiant, smug grin.

"How was that for an exhibition?" she asks.

"Fine, fine," I say. "You made your point. Perhaps air magic has some bite after all."

"Of course it does!" Melindra says, puffing out her chest. "Ordinary wind causes destruction, but this is pure compressed magic. You can hone the edges sharp as steel, weave kinetic barriers, compress the pressure—do you have any idea how many applications—"

Her voice cuts off. Her knees buckle, her eyelids fluttering shut as she pitches forward toward the turf.

"Hold on!" Daren catches her by the elbows, steadying her before she hits the dirt. "Are you alright?"

"I am fine," Melindra mumbles, pressing a hand against her forehead, her eyes remaining squeezed shut.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Tornado2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Tornado2)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Tornado2
TEXT:

"Hah!" Arraka's laugh rings through the clearing. "What did I tell you? Zero restraint! She dumped her entire reservoir into that parlor trick, and now she can barely keep her boots under her!"

"Are you serious?" Daren asks, keeping a firm grip on Melindra's shoulder to keep her upright. "Should we stop to let you rest?"

"I said I am fine!" Melindra snaps, jerking her shoulder out of his grasp. "Keep moving."

We trade uneasy glances, but no one argues. For the first twenty minutes through the timber, her stride is visibly unsteady, her boots catching on roots and exposed rock. But as the miles pass, her footing firms up, her reserves steadily replenishing from the ambient mana.

"So," I say, stepping alongside her as we pick our way past the craters and snapped timber left by her twister. "Remember our conversation yesterday? You were right on the verge of explaining how stillwaters are created before we got sidetracked. Shall we pick up where we left off?"

"That is a fabrication and you know it," Melindra says without looking at me. "I never promised to reveal how stillwaters are made."

"You never promised in writing," I say. "But you will tell us, right?"

"No."

"Why not?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Trailing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Trailing)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Whatever
TEXT:

"As you like," I say.

"Excellent," Daren says, clapping his hands together. "Let us move out. Melindra, lead the way."

"Right," Melindra says. "As I was saying before the interruptions, cutting through the forest shaves hours off the march. Stay alert for monsters. There are no city patrols in these woods. Follow me."

We trek through the dense canopy in silence for several minutes. Once the initial irritation clears and our pace settles, I step up beside her to resume probing.

"So," I say to Melindra. "Remember our conversation yesterday? You were right on the verge of explaining how stillwaters are created before we got sidetracked. Shall we pick up where we left off?"

"That is a fabrication and you know it," Melindra says without looking at me. "I never promised to reveal how stillwaters are made."

"You never promised in writing," I say. "But you will tell us, right?"

"No."

"Why not?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Trailing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Trailing)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Trailing
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch8_trivia,1) if (v_b2_ch8_tickle == 0)
achievement("Stillwater trivia",v_ac_b2_ch8_trivia)
#if(v_b2_ch8_tickle == 0) {
"Because Eiden decreed after the still winter that the event remains buried," Melindra says. "He feared that if ordinary mages learned how we were made, people would butcher half the continent trying to duplicate the process."

"Hold on," Hadrik says, ducking beneath a hanging vine. "Are you saying every stillwater in the world was born during the still winter?"

"Essentially," Melindra says. "I doubt you will find a stillwater alive today under six centuries old."

"Are all stillwaters immortal, then?" Kate asks, her gaze fixed on the trail ahead.

"Not immortal," Melindra says. "A blade through the throat kills us just as dead as anyone else. We simply ceased aging the day the transformation took hold. It left us sterile and immune to nearly every sickness, but that is secondary."

"None of you can bear children?" Hadrik asks.

"That is what sterile means, dwarf."

"Where are the rest?" Daren asks.

"In the dirt," Melindra says flatly. "Eiden executed the vast majority. A handful lost their minds to the power and were hunted down by mercenary guilds. There might be a few hermits I forgot, but I have not crossed paths with another stillwater besides Eiden in centuries. He roamed the four continents for generations, so most tavern songs you hear about stillwater terrors are about him."

"Eiden slaughtered his own kind?" Daren's jaw tightens. "Why? When?"

"Right," Melindra says, holding up a hand. "I have already shared far more than I should have. If you want the bloody details, take them up with Eiden yourself."

"Tell us your connection to him, at least," Kate says. "Are you kin?"

"We are not kin," Melindra says.

"Former lovers, then?" Hadrik asks with a grin.

"Hardly," Melindra says.

"Then why did Arraka claim you trailed behind him and the golden fox through the still winter?" Kate presses.

"We struck up an acquaintance while he was imprisoned," Melindra says. "Trading partners, strictly speaking. The friendship came later."
}
#if(v_b2_ch8_tickle == 1) {
"If you wanted history lessons," Melindra says, "you should have considered that before making an enemy out of me."

"An enemy?" I say. "Are you still nursing a grudge over five seconds of ribs-poking?"

"Yes," Melindra says coldly.

"A harmless prank," I say. "Give us a hint, at least."

"Save your breath," Melindra says. "Not another syllable."

"Tell us your connection to him, at least," Kate says. "Are you kin?"

"We are not kin," Melindra says.

"Former lovers, then?" Hadrik asks with a grin.

"Hardly," Melindra says.

"Then why did Arraka claim you trailed behind him and the golden fox through the still winter?" Kate presses.

"We struck up an acquaintance while he was imprisoned," Melindra says. "Trading partners, strictly speaking. The friendship came later."
}
choice(""Was this before the still winter? What was he being imprisoned for?"", B2-Ch08a-Imprisoned, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Imprisoned)
choice(""How did he manage to get imprisoned when he has all these powers?"", B2-Ch08a-Powers, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Powers)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Imprisoned
TEXT:

"Was this before the still winter? What was he being imprisoned for?"

"Before the winter, yes," Melindra says. "As for the charges, I have no idea. He never volunteered them."

"Who was his jailer?" Kate asks.

Melindra's stride falters slightly. "I cannot discuss that."

"On Eiden's orders?" Kate presses.

"Something along those lines," Melindra says.

"What about the trading arrangement?" Hadrik asks. "Can you share that?"

"I suppose," Melindra says, her tone lightening. "My duties included sweeping the dungeon corridors. There was a heap of loose stone piled in the corner of his cell. Every rock had such fascinating striations, fractures, and mineral veins that I could hardly tear my eyes away. I needed those specimens for my collection. So I lingered by his bars every morning, striking up conversation to uncover what he lacked, hoping to barter supplies for stone."

"You traded provisions for ordinary rocks?" Daren asks, incredulous. "Could you not simply ask him for a handful?"

"Of course I could have asked!" Melindra snaps. "What fool surrenders valuable mineral specimens for nothing?"

"They are—" Daren starts, then pinches the bridge of his nose and exhales slowly. "Never mind. My apologies. Continue."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Trade, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Trade)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Powers
TEXT:

"How did he manage to get imprisoned when he has all these powers?"

"He was not a stillwater back then," Melindra says.

"He was still a practicing mage," Hadrik says. "How did they suppress his casting? Seredium plating? A paralysis rune?"

"An ordinary iron-barred cell," Melindra says.

"Was he collared?" Kate asks.

"No," Melindra says thoughtfully. "No collar."

"Then how did they hold him?" Kate asks. "And who captured him in the first place?"

Melindra's expression tightens. "I cannot discuss that."

"On Eiden's orders?" Kate presses.

"Something along those lines," Melindra says.

"What about the trading arrangement?" Hadrik asks. "Can you share that?"

"I suppose," Melindra says, her tone lightening. "My duties included sweeping the dungeon corridors. There was a heap of loose stone piled in the corner of his cell. Every rock had such fascinating striations, fractures, and mineral veins that I could hardly tear my eyes away. I needed those specimens for my collection. So I lingered by his bars every morning, striking up conversation to uncover what he lacked, hoping to barter supplies for stone."

"You traded provisions for ordinary rocks?" Daren asks, incredulous. "Could you not simply ask him for a handful?"

"Of course I could have asked!" Melindra snaps. "What fool surrenders valuable mineral specimens for nothing?"

"They are—" Daren starts, then pinches the bridge of his nose and exhales slowly. "Never mind. My apologies. Continue."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Trade, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Trade)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Trade
TEXT:

"Thank you," Melindra says. "As I was saying: dungeon rations were slop. Since I assisted in the scullery, I offered to smuggle roast meat and fresh bread into his cell in exchange for specific stones from his pile. At first he treated the bargain as a joke. When I returned with a warm pie and held out my palm, he stared at me in disbelief. I had to point out the exact piece of quartz before he handed it over. He laughed every time I called our exchanges an even trade. I never grasped the humor, but he honored the terms."

A sudden realization clicks into place. During our confrontation in the city, I offered Eiden my own version of an "even trade"—and he roared with laughter. Now I know the joke he was remembering.

"You collect stones?" Flower pipes up, bouncing on her heels. "Do you have them in your pack right now?"

"Certainly," Melindra says. "The collection has expanded over six centuries, but the original dungeon pieces are right here. Would you like to inspect them?"

"Yes!" Flower's eyes shine. "Show me!"

"Keep pace, then," Melindra says. "I am not halting the march. Walk with me."

"Coming!" Flower scurries to her side.

Melindra unslings her leather pack, withdrawing rough chunks of granite, slate, and river rock one by one, handing them to Flower with earnest explanations of their cleavage and mineral composition. To an appraiser, the collection is worthless rubble, but Flower marvels at every fracture while Melindra beams with collector's pride.

Daren gives a discreet hand sign. The rest of us drop back several paces, letting the two of them pull ahead out of earshot.

"What is our read on her so far?" Daren murmurs, keeping his voice low under the rustle of the canopy. "Could she be working as Eiden's agent?"

choice(""I think that she's a bit stuck up, but I doubt she's a traitor."", B2-Ch08a-Stuck, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Stuck)
choice(""Nah, she seems alright to me."", B2-Ch08a-Alright, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Alright)
choice(""You mean, like an informant? It's certainly possible."", B2-Ch08a-Informant, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Informant)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Stuck
TEXT:

"I think that she's a bit stuck up, but I doubt she's a traitor."

"Daren, you are seeing shadows," Hadrik says. "We knew she had ties to Eiden before we accepted her escort. No need to interrogate every breath she takes."

"You are missing the tactical reality," Kate says, her eyes sweeping the perimeter. "Her trustworthiness is irrelevant. So long as we withhold sensitive plans in her presence, we remain secure."

"It is entirely relevant!" Daren whispers harshly. "Huddling in the rear every time we need to exchange three words screams conspiracy!"

"So what?" Kate says. "Why should her suspicions concern us?"

"Because she is traveling as part of our team!" Daren says.

Kate raises an eyebrow. "Is she?"

"My question is whether she warrants our confidence," Daren says. "If the policy is absolute secrecy, then by definition—"

"You know," Melindra calls back over her shoulder, watching our tight cluster from twenty paces ahead, "if you intend to conspire against me, you could at least attempt subtlety."

"What?" Daren's voice cracks into an agonizingly transparent pitch. "No! You misunderstand entirely! We were discussing... ah... Flower!"

Flower looks back, her brow furrowing with worry. "You were?"

"Masterful recovery, Daren," I say, thumping him on his pauldrons. "No one could possibly suspect intrigue after an explanation of that caliber."

"Be quiet," Daren mutters under his breath.

"Hold your council at camp," Melindra says, scanning the treeline. "Lagging behind forces me to track your positions while monitoring the monster pack moving to intercept us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Defenses, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Defenses)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Alright
TEXT:

"Nah, she seems alright to me."

"Hauling thirty pounds of river gravel across the provinces is a strange habit," Hadrik says, "but I doubt she is an informant. We knew she had ties to Eiden before we accepted her escort. No need to interrogate every breath she takes."

"You are missing the tactical reality," Kate says, her eyes sweeping the perimeter. "Her trustworthiness is irrelevant. So long as we withhold sensitive plans in her presence, we remain secure."

"It is entirely relevant!" Daren whispers harshly. "Huddling in the rear every time we need to exchange three words screams conspiracy!"

"So what?" Kate says. "Why should her suspicions concern us?"

"Because she is traveling as part of our team!" Daren says.

Kate raises an eyebrow. "Is she?"

"My question is whether she warrants our confidence," Daren says. "If the policy is absolute secrecy, then by definition—"

"You know," Melindra calls back over her shoulder, watching our tight cluster from twenty paces ahead, "if you intend to conspire against me, you could at least attempt subtlety."

"What?" Daren's voice cracks into an agonizingly transparent pitch. "No! You misunderstand entirely! We were discussing... ah... Flower!"

Flower looks back, her brow furrowing with worry. "You were?"

"Masterful recovery, Daren," I say, thumping him on his pauldrons. "No one could possibly suspect intrigue after an explanation of that caliber."

"Be quiet," Daren mutters under his breath.

"Hold your council at camp," Melindra says, scanning the treeline. "Lagging behind forces me to track your positions while monitoring the monster pack moving to intercept us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Defenses, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Defenses)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Informant
TEXT:

"You mean, like an informant? It's certainly possible."

"Come off it," Hadrik says. "She shares a harmless anecdote about bartering for gravel, and you brand her a spy? We knew she had ties to Eiden before we accepted her escort. Why does her origin story change the board?"

"You are missing the tactical reality," Kate says, her eyes sweeping the perimeter. "Her trustworthiness is irrelevant. So long as we withhold sensitive plans in her presence, we remain secure."

"It is entirely relevant!" Daren whispers harshly. "Huddling in the rear every time we need to exchange three words screams conspiracy!"

"So what?" Kate says. "Why should her suspicions concern us?"

"Because she is traveling as part of our team!" Daren says.

Kate raises an eyebrow. "Is she?"

"My question is whether she warrants our confidence," Daren says. "If the policy is absolute secrecy, then by definition—"

"You know," Melindra calls back over her shoulder, watching our tight cluster from twenty paces ahead, "if you intend to conspire against me, you could at least attempt subtlety."

"What?" Daren's voice cracks into an agonizingly transparent pitch. "No! You misunderstand entirely! We were discussing... ah... Flower!"

Flower looks back, her brow furrowing with worry. "You were?"

"Masterful recovery, Daren," I say, thumping him on his pauldrons. "No one could possibly suspect intrigue after an explanation of that caliber."

"Be quiet," Daren mutters under his breath.

"Hold your council at camp," Melindra says, scanning the treeline. "Lagging behind forces me to track your positions while monitoring the monster pack moving to intercept us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Defenses, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Defenses)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Defenses
TEXT:

"An ambush?" I shrug my backpack off and haul the crossbow free. "Arraka, you had one job."

"My task is detecting invisible threats, fool," Arraka snarls from the amulet. "Every one of these beasts possesses a physical form. What sort of half-wits walk blindly into an ambush on dry ground?"

"What are we facing?" Kate asks, her boots shifting into a ready stance. "And how many? They are suppressing their auras too tightly for my magical senses."

"A short distance out," Melindra says casually. "Five orcs, two ogres, two goblins. The goblins are casters; the others carry heavy iron. You should manage them without trouble."

"'You'?" Daren turns to her. "Do you mean 'we'? As in the group traveling under a joint contract?"

"Of course not," Melindra says. "What use are you in Ollendor if five orcs run you down in an open forest? Do you know what kind of garrison protects the royal district? How do you imagine their king defied every sovereign in Varathia and survived?"

"If they butcher us here, we never reach Ollendor," Daren says.

"Then I hire better mercenaries," Melindra says with a shrug. "The ice mage can handle her flank. The girl with the daggers is fast. Even the half-lessathi should hold his own if his stats are allocated properly."

"We have names," Daren says.

"Your names are irrelevant," Melindra says. "Races and disciplines distinguish you perfectly well."

"You go out of your way to be obnoxious, do you not?" Daren mutters.

"Silence!" Kate snaps. "Ogres in the rear. Heavy footfalls. Orcs advancing through the front brush."

"Impressive," Melindra says, leaning forward. "You distinguish species by ground vibrations? Is it the mass disparity?"

"Shut your mouth!" Kate says. "If you refuse to fight, get clear of the line and stop talking."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Flaming, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Flaming)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Flaming
TEXT:

"Touchy," Melindra murmurs. "I wonder if there really is a link between elemental affinity and temperament."

"Do you ever stop?" Kate raises her left palm. A disc of frost materializes before her chest an instant before an iron arrow buries itself in the ice with a dull thud.

"For Tyrath!" a guttural roar erupts from the thicket.

Five green-skinned brutes burst through the brush in a dead sprint, roaring in unison. They stand as tall as Daren, corded with thick muscle beneath plate armor. Their leader charges with two double-bladed battleaxes wreathed in roaring fire. Behind him, one wields a massive two-handed greatsword, while the remaining three advance behind heavy seredium-rimmed heater shields and spiked maces.

The axe-wielding captain slams headlong into Daren's raised shield, sparks showering across the moss.

"Dragon elite squad!" Leila's glowing blue letters flare between the trunks. "Do not engage lightly!"

She slips behind the least armored orc like a shadow, driving twin daggers deep beneath his shoulder blades. The brute bellows, spinning to bring his mace across in a brutal horizontal sweep, but Leila is already gone, sliding out of his reach.

"Leila, flanking!" Kate warns.

A second mace-wielding orc lunges for Leila's flank. Kate drives a jagged pillar of ice up through the loam beneath his boots. The orc's enchanted mace shatters the frost in an explosion of white shards, his momentum broken just as Leila rolls clear.

"Ogres are mine!" Hadrik roars, charging the two towering shapes crashing through the rear brush.

Flower unleashes a column of white-hot flame directly into the broadsword orc. The fire washes over him, parting uselessly against his anti-magic armor. He strides out of the inferno, lifting the massive blade to cleave her in two.

"Move!" Flower dives sideways, tumbling across exposed roots.

I plant my boots, center the crosshairs on the slit in the orc's helmet, and pull the trigger.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Crossbow, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Crossbow)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Crossbow
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch8_savior,1) if (v_b2_ch6_crossbow == 0 && v_b2_ch8_tornado == 0 && v_combat_technique == 0 && v_reflexes < 2)
