ID: B2-Ch08a-Goblins
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch8_effects,1) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 0)
achievement("Side effects",v_ac_b2_ch8_effects)
#if(v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 0) {
"Flower! Illuna!" I call out. "We need to eliminate the goblin mages. Cover me! Arraka, give us a vector!"

"Right, the casters!" Flower says. "I felt an incantation when the charge started. This way!"

We plunge into the dense thicket, leaving the clash of steel behind. We sprint through the ferns for twenty seconds, but the canopy feels unchanged, every mossy trunk blurring into the last.

"What are you doing?" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "Can you primates not maintain a heading for ten yards? You have been running in a circle!"

"We ran straight north!" I pant.

"You ran in a ring, moron! Look!"

A miniature glowing map ignites in the air between us, showing a red trail that loops back on itself in an endless knot around two static green dots.

"See?" Arraka mocks. "That is your brilliant navigation. The targets are right there."

I fix my eyes on the coordinate and push forward through the briars, but after four paces, my thoughts drift. A thick fog wraps around my mind, an overwhelming urge insisting that there is nothing ahead, that I must turn around and inspect the rear trail. My boots pivot on their own.

"A suggestion ward," Illuna says, her eyes narrowing as her colder voice takes over. "Arraka is insulated inside the seredium housing, but the field operates like Kate's perimeter wards."

"Right," I say, shaking the cobwebs from my skull. "The dizziness ward lowered my resistance to mental compulsions. The urge to turn back hits like a physical wall."

"Sleep and vertigo wards remain far more common," Illuna says. "A reasonable trade. We cannot bypass this barrier without visualizing the precise terrain around the casters. Arraka, describe their clearing."
}
#if(v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1) {
"Flower! Illuna!" I call out. "We need to eliminate the goblin mages. Cover me! Arraka, give us a vector!"

"Right, the casters!" Flower says. "I felt an incantation when the charge started. This way!"

We plunge into the dense thicket, leaving the clash of steel behind. We sprint through the ferns for twenty seconds, but the canopy feels unchanged, every mossy trunk blurring into the last.

"What are you doing?" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "Can you primates not maintain a heading for ten yards? You have been running in a circle!"

"We ran straight north!" I pant.

"You ran in a ring, moron! Look!"

A miniature glowing map ignites in the air between us, showing a red trail that loops back on itself in an endless knot around two static green dots.

"See?" Arraka mocks. "That is your brilliant navigation. The targets are right there."

I fix my eyes on the coordinate and push forward through the briars, but after four paces, my boots unconsciously veer sideways, steering me away from the center.

"A suggestion ward," Illuna says, her eyes narrowing as her colder voice takes over. "Arraka is insulated inside the seredium housing, but the field operates like Kate's perimeter wards. We cannot bypass this barrier without visualizing the precise terrain around the casters. Arraka, describe their clearing."
}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Goblins2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Goblins2)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Goblins2
TEXT:

A brass horn sounds from the clearing behind us, sharp and piercing.

Before Arraka can answer, the two green dots vanish from her holographic map. In the same breath, the heavy magical signatures of the orcs and ogres blink out of existence.

"They had their roles assigned," Illuna says. "One anchored the suggestion field while the second prepared a mass teleportation incantation under cover."

"Back to the clearing," I say.

We break through the brush to find the combat over, but not without cost. Leila sits in the dirt, her left ankle badly swollen and purple. Daren kneels over her, channeling bright white light from his palms into the shattered joint.

"Hold steady," Daren murmurs gently. Leila grits her teeth, her knuckles white against her knees without uttering a sound.

"What happened?" Flower gasps.

"Leila felled two of her opponents," Daren says, not looking up from the wound. "The third caught her leg with a mace before Kate put an ice cone through his visor. Their captain blew the signal the moment his squad broke. Did the goblins escape?"

"Teleported the entire unit," Illuna says. "Their ward held us at bay."

"Where did they retreat?" Hadrik asks, wiping sweat from his brow.

"Out of range," Arraka says. "Gone back to their war camp, wherever Tyrath keeps his barracks."

"Bone is set," Daren says as the swelling recedes and the skin clears. "Tendon needs rest. No sprinting or acrobatics for a day, but you can walk."

Leila traces glowing blue letters in the air: "Thank you."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Dispatches, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Dispatches)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Trees
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar != 0) {
"Flower! Illuna!" I say. "Find the goblin casters! Arraka can guide you!"

"Right!" Flower says. "Come on, Petal!"

As they disappear into the brush, I draw my scimitar. With level three strength, I sever a young oak trunk in a single clean sweep, sheathe the blade, and hoist the twelve-foot log onto my shoulder like a caber.

The combatants in the clearing freeze. Orcs, ogres, even Daren and Kate stare at me in stunned silence.

I sprint straight at the three mace-wielders and slam the heavy trunk down. The timber crushes the lead orc into the earth with a sickening crunch.

"Leila, clear out!" I shout.
}
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0) {
"Flower! Illuna!" I say. "Find the goblin casters! Arraka can guide you!"

"Right!" Flower says. "Come on, Petal!"

As they disappear into the brush, I draw my dagger. Working quickly, I hack through the base of a young oak, severing the trunk with brute strength, and lift the twelve-foot log onto my shoulder.

The combatants in the clearing freeze. Orcs, ogres, even Daren and Kate stare at me in stunned silence.

I sprint straight at the three mace-wielders and slam the heavy trunk down. The timber crushes the lead orc into the earth with a sickening crunch.

"Leila, clear out!" I shout.
}
Leila vaults backward, putting distance between herself and the remaining two orcs. I swing the tree trunk like a massive club. The heavy wood catches the second orc in his ribs, shattering plate and bone and launching him ten feet into the brush. The third orc turns to pursue Leila, but she reverses direction instantly, driving her dagger across his throat.

"AHAHAHA!" Hadrik roars with delight. "Brilliant!"

He grabs a pine tree behind him and yanks it clean out of the dirt, roots showering soil everywhere. He brings it down on the nearest ogre; the beast raises his club, the sheer force driving his boots a foot into the mud.

"Daren, heads up!" I call.

Daren leaps clear as I swing the massive trunk at the orc captain.

The captain does not flinch. His flaming axes blur through the air, carving the trunk into six burning chunks that scatter across the turf before the blow connects.

He glares at me over the smoking stump in my hands.

Instead of lunging, he reaches for a war horn at his collar and blows a sharp, blaring note. The ogres disengage from Hadrik as a blinding white light engulfs the surviving monsters, their outlines vibrating into nothingness.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Retreat, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Retreat)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Fight
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility >= 3 || v_reflexes >= 2)
