ID: B2-Ch08a-Retreat
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch8_tree,1) if (v_b2_ch8_goblins == 2)
achievement("Tree wielder",v_ac_b2_ch8_tree)
#if(v_b2_ch8_goblins == 0) {
"They are escaping!" Daren charges forward, but the teleportation takes hold before he can strike.

The clearing falls quiet, the ground littered with the three dead orcs.

"The goblins were tactical anchors," Kate says, stepping over a severed mace. "Holding the teleportation matrix in reserve."

Flower emerges from the thicket, breathing hard. "Did they vanish? Oh—you killed the three with the shields..."

"Captain and ogres extracted," Illuna says.

"What stalled you on the casters?" I ask.

"A suggestion ward," Illuna says. "It repelled us until the retreat horn sounded."

"Will they return?" I ask.

"Not today," Illuna says. "Arraka reports no auras within miles. They fell back to their command post."
}
#if(v_b2_ch8_goblins == 2) {
"They are escaping!" Daren charges forward, but the teleportation takes hold before he can strike.

The clearing falls quiet, the ground littered with the three dead orcs.

"The goblins were tactical anchors," Kate says, stepping over a severed mace. "Holding the teleportation matrix in reserve."

Flower emerges from the thicket, breathing hard. "Did they vanish? Oh—you killed the three with the shields..."

"Captain and ogres extracted," Illuna says.

"What stalled you on the casters?" I ask.

Flower blinks at the splintered log in my hands, taking an involuntary step back. "What... are you holding?"

"A suggestion ward," Illuna answers my question. "It repelled us until the retreat horn sounded."

"Will they return?" I ask.

"Not today," Illuna says. "Arraka reports no auras within miles. They fell back to their command post."

"As entertaining as this lumberjack routine was," Daren says, looking between Hadrik and me, "could you kindly discard the timber? You are making me uneasy."

"Right," Hadrik and I say together, tossing our trees aside.

"I would prefer we avoid improvised forestry in future skirmishes," Daren adds. "Tracking four hostiles is difficult enough without dodging thirty-foot branches."

"Just having fun," Hadrik laughs. "I punch harder with bare fists anyway."

"Still," Daren tells me with a grin, "not a bad swing, Barry."

"Thanks," I say. "You held your own."
}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Dispatches, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Dispatches)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Dispatches
TEXT:

"Those ogres were remarkably disciplined," Hadrik says, rolling his shoulders. "Nearly as stubborn as the chieftain at the redoubt. And their axe captain held Daren to a dead standstill."

"They were organized," I say, stepping over broken branches. "Not ordinary wandering beasts. Leila, you identified them as dragon elites?"

"Yes," Leila writes, the blue light fading as we walk. "Tyrath deploys these units exclusively for first-marked targets. He cannot hunt you personally under the non-interference pact with Eiden, so he stationed squads along the city's perimeter roads to intercept your departure."

choice(""Do all of the elite groups have the same team composition?"", B2-Ch08a-Composition, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Composition)
choice(""Does Tyrath have any teams that are stronger than these elite squads?"", B2-Ch08a-Stronger, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Stronger)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Composition
TEXT:

"Do all of the elite groups have the same team composition?"

"No," Leila writes. "Squad loadouts vary. Some units ride young wyverns for aerial strikes; others deploy regenerative river trolls as vanguard shock troops."

"Regardless of their roster," Illuna says, "our primary concern is early detection. Arraka, you will report all approaching formations immediately, physical or incorporeal. Is that clear?"

"If you want scout reports, primate, learn to cast under pressure," Arraka snarls from the gemstone. "What was that pathetic showing in the brush? Why hold back the drowning sphere I taught you? Anti-magic steel does not filter water from lungs."

"You know the constraints," Illuna says coldly. "The technique is unstable. Landing it on the dragon was a desperate gamble timed to his intake of breath. Had he retained air in his chest, a single exhale would have ruptured the sphere. The matrix binds to a single target, and any broken focus dissolves the spell instantly."

"Then drill it until it holds!" Arraka snaps. "I gave you a lethal art and you leave it to rot on the shelf. Do you hamstring your own repertoire purely to spite your teacher?"

"Fine," Illuna says. "I will practice the technique if it buys your silence. Do we have your agreement on reconnaissance?"

"Certainly," Arraka laughs softly. "Keeping your wretched host alive serves my interests until I reclaim the vessel."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Inspiring, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Inspiring)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Stronger
TEXT:

"Does Tyrath have any teams that are stronger than these elite squads?"

"Historically," Leila writes. "Not currently. He once commanded flights of lesser drakes, before his core destabilized and cost him his permanent magma form. The surviving wild dragons of Varathia sequestered themselves in the northern crags and reject his authority entirely."

"Regardless of their roster," Illuna says, "our primary concern is early detection. Arraka, you will report all approaching formations immediately, physical or incorporeal. Is that clear?"

"If you want scout reports, primate, learn to cast under pressure," Arraka snarls from the gemstone. "What was that pathetic showing in the brush? Why hold back the drowning sphere I taught you? Anti-magic steel does not filter water from lungs."

"You know the constraints," Illuna says coldly. "The technique is unstable. Landing it on the dragon was a desperate gamble timed to his intake of breath. Had he retained air in his chest, a single exhale would have ruptured the sphere. The matrix binds to a single target, and any broken focus dissolves the spell instantly."

"Then drill it until it holds!" Arraka snaps. "I gave you a lethal art and you leave it to rot on the shelf. Do you hamstring your own repertoire purely to spite your teacher?"

"Fine," Illuna says. "I will practice the technique if it buys your silence. Do we have your agreement on reconnaissance?"

"Certainly," Arraka laughs softly. "Keeping your wretched host alive serves my interests until I reclaim the vessel."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Inspiring, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Inspiring)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Inspiring
TEXT:

"Daren," I say, glancing at the seredium gorget resting against his tunic. "Your plate incorporates anti-magic enchantments, correct?"

"Among other wards, yes," Daren says.

"Why skip the helm?" I ask. "You watched those orcs shrug off spells with full-faced iron. A closed visor hardly adds much weight to a sixty-pound suit."

"Mass is not the issue," Daren says. "Awareness is. Brutes trade their peripheral vision for an extra layer of iron, but in a chaotic melee with flanking threats, blinding your own corners is suicide. Furthermore, the cross on my forehead is recognizable across the eastern provinces. When hostile mercenaries identify the mark, they frequently reconsider drawing steel, which saves blood on both sides."

"How did you get the cross?" Hadrik asks, leaning in. "A beast with crossed claws, or an arena wager?"

"Neither," Daren says. "Two distinct cuts from a single duel."

Hadrik's eyebrows shoot up. "Someone landed two clean strikes on the great paladin Daren? Who held the blade?"

"My martial and enchanting master," Daren says, his tone softening with memory. "An ex-sage of the Southern Continent. He was an unyielding instructor who believed painful experience taught faster than lectures. For my final trial, he ordered me to fight him with everything I possessed. That was when he marked me. The first cut opened my brow because I failed to recognize the tactical flaw he was exposing. When I failed to adjust, he struck the exact same point from the opposing angle, crossing the wound. Only then did I understand the gaping vulnerability in my guard that I had neglected for years. On his third attempt, I parried and disarmed him. Now, whenever I see my reflection in a pool, the scar reminds me that overlooking small weaknesses gets a man killed."

"Oddly inspiring," Hadrik rumbles, rubbing his jaw. "My giant hide makes me careless about minor openings. Perhaps I should dedicate a few evenings to refining my guard before an enemy finds the seam."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Uphold, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Uphold)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Uphold
TEXT:

"Hey!" Melindra's voice drifts down from twenty feet above the branches. "Are we finished with the war council? May I descend to resume our march? No rush, of course."

"Yes, Melindra," Daren calls up, sighing. "You can come down."

She drops through the foliage, touching the moss with a light tap of her boots. "That skirmish was embarrassing. If this is your standard, you will be useless inside Ollendor."

"Melindra," Daren says, planting his boots firmly before her. "You swore an oath to deliver us to our contact. We took no oath to remain in your company. We can dissolve this arrangement at any milestone. If you require our blades in Ollendor, cease the insults and guide the march. If not, find another party to haul your freight."

"Fine," Melindra mutters, scowling deeply. "Not another word."

She pivots and marches ahead into the deep timber.

"Illuna," I say quietly as we follow her stride. "Could those goblin casters belong to Fyron's faction? Is the general aligned with the dragon?"

"Doubtful," Illuna says. "Tyrath commands legions of subjugated goblins. He has no need for Fyron's independent band. Fyron's enclave survives largely because the dragon considers them beneath his attention. No pact shields them; Tyrath could scorch their warrens in an afternoon if he chose."

choice(""Did Fyron ever serve under the dragon, before he joined the free goblins?"", B2-Ch08a-Serve, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Serve)
choice(""How many types of monsters does Tyrath have under his rule, exactly?"", B2-Ch08a-Types, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Types)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Serve
TEXT:

"Did Fyron ever serve under the dragon, before he joined the free goblins?"

"Decades ago," Illuna says. "He deserted over the systematic abuse of his kin. Goblins possess natural resourcefulness, but their physical frame is frail and innate spellcasters are rare among them. The dragon's commanders relegated them to menial labor and starvation rations. When Fyron led his desertion into the hills, Tyrath did not bother pursuing them. Fyron established the Free Goblins on a charter of self-determination. Many perished in the early winters, and some returned to Tyrath for basic sustenance, but the core survived, gradually recruiting deserters and outcasts from other tribes."

"Wait," Daren interrupts, frowning between us. "Who is this Fyron, and why do you speak of him as an acquaintance?"

"General of the independent goblin warrens," Illuna says.

"When did you cross paths with a monster general?" Daren asks.

"Illuna knew him previously," I say. "Leila and I met him in the arena maintenance tunnels while sabotaging the tournament matches."

Daren freezes, staring at me. "You were sabotaging the—" He cuts himself off, rubbing his temples. "Gods above. Nothing with you is ever straightforward, Barry."

"Tactics, gentlemen," Hadrik interjects. "Tyrath's elite squads are tracking our heading. Our magic glanced off their armor, and their coordination was seamless. We need tighter battle plans."

"A certain stillwater drawing steel would help," Kate says dryly.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Capabilities, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Capabilities)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Types
TEXT:

"How many types of monsters does Tyrath have under his rule, exactly?"

"Orcs form the core of his standing army," Illuna says. "They are disciplined, formidable, and fiercely loyal. He supplements them with ogre shock troops, mercenary troll clans, goblin skirmishers, and trained war beasts like wyverns and hydras. Wild ogres roam independently, and troll allegiances shift with gold, but nearly every orc on Varathia answers to the dragon."

"Wait," Daren interrupts, frowning between us. "Who is this Fyron, and why do you speak of him as an acquaintance?"

"General of the independent goblin warrens," Illuna says.

"When did you cross paths with a monster general?" Daren asks.

"Illuna knew him previously," I say. "Leila and I met him in the arena maintenance tunnels while sabotaging the tournament matches."

Daren freezes, staring at me. "You were sabotaging the—" He cuts himself off, rubbing his temples. "Gods above. Nothing with you is ever straightforward, Barry."

"Tactics, gentlemen," Hadrik interjects. "Tyrath's elite squads are tracking our heading. Our magic glanced off their armor, and their coordination was seamless. We need tighter battle plans."

"A certain stillwater drawing steel would help," Kate says dryly.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Capabilities, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Capabilities)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Capabilities
TEXT:

"Drop it, Kate," Daren says. "Bickering changes nothing, and Melindra is not our permanent responsibility. Hadrik has the right of it—our formation fell apart. Leila, when you draw fire, keep your distance rather than locking into melee. Drawing three blades at once forces our casters to drop their targets to peel enemies off you."

Leila traces glowing blue letters in the air: "Sorry..."

"No need for apologies," Daren says gently. "The instinct to pull pressure off the line is sound, but we need you mobile. Keep them chasing you at range. That leaves Kate and Flower free to crack the unarmored targets."

Leila writes: "Understood. I will adjust."

"And Kate," Daren continues, "Hadrik holding two ogres does not mean leaving him isolated. A single ice cone into an ogre's flank ends that fight in five seconds, and Hadrik sweeps the rest of the field. Stop hovering over Leila. She is a seasoned fighter; trust her to keep her feet."

Kate keeps her eyes on the trail, jaw set. "Understood."

Melindra glances back over her shoulder, eyebrow raised. "Surprisingly competent tactical assessment from someone who spent the battle trading blows with axes. Perhaps I underestimated your discipline."

"Twenty years on the eastern frontiers teaches a man a few lessons," Daren says. "I thought you promised silence?"

"I was thinking aloud," Melindra says, tossing her ponytail as she turns back. "Do not flatter yourself."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Bothered, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Bothered)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Bothered
TEXT:

"Does that mean you will not show me the rest of your rocks?" Flower asks, her shoulders drooping with disappointment.

"I suppose I can make an exception," Melindra says, her expression softening. "Just for you. Come along."

Flower hurries back to her side, and we resume our trek under the green canopy. For the next three hours, no monsters disturb our line. Melindra speaks to no one but Flower, and the rest of us maintain our distance. We drift into natural pairs: Kate and Leila trailing twenty paces back, speaking in low, steady murmurs as they bridge ten years of silence; Hadrik, Daren, and I holding the center; and Flower bouncing along the vanguard beside Melindra.

As Flower finishes inspecting the minerals, Melindra pivots to probing questions regarding the dual-soul fusion. She asks about sensory overlap, consciousness handoffs, and mana sharing. Some inquiries border on invasive, but Flower answers each with sunny candor while Melindra listens with genuine, unfeigned curiosity.

Past midday, we halt beside a clear tributary to draw water, forage berries, and share dried trail meat, sparing Flower the mana drain of conjuring lunch. Illuna's conjured water dissolves into thin air within minutes of leaving her hands, making physical mountain streams our only reliable hydration.

When I finish my ration, I settle onto a flat boulder and crack open my notebook, scanning my margin notes on Eiden's spell mechanics.

Out of the corner of my eye, I watch Melindra pace toward my perch. She hesitates twice, adjusting her sleeves, before stopping squarely in front of my boots.

"So... Barry, was it?" Melindra puts on an exaggerated, sugary smile. "How fares your afternoon?"

"You managed to learn my name," I say without lifting my eyes from the page. "Remarkable progress."

"Your name is simpler than the others," Melindra says.

choice(""It is indeed."", B2-Ch08a-Indeed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Indeed)
choice(""Is this the part where you make fun of how common my name is?"", B2-Ch08a-Common, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Common)
choice(""That's funny... Because you were calling me 'half-lessathi' just a few hours ago..."", B2-Ch08a-Funny, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Funny)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Indeed
TEXT:

"It is, indeed," I say, turning a leaf in my notebook.

A heavy silence settles between us. I trace a diagram with my thumb.

"I was curious about something," Melindra says, leaning against a birch trunk. "You are half-lessathi, yet you entered this contest from across the ocean. Lessathi settlements exist exclusively on Varathia. Who were your parents, and why did they cross the sea?"

"If you mean my blood parents, I never laid eyes on them," I say. "The couple who raised me on the farm were ordinary humans."

"Were?" Melindra asks, catching the tense. "Not are?"

"They are dead," I say flatly. "Killed by a banshee."

Nearby, the scrape of Daren's whetstone against his broadsword slows. He listens without turning his head. I never detailed that morning to him.

"A banshee killed them?" Melindra asks. "And you survived?"

"I was there," I say. "I killed her."

"You killed an awakened banshee without magic?" Melindra stares at me. "The only window a mortal possesses to harm a banshee is during the immediate trauma of the transformation, before the ethereal link solidifies. Which implies..."

She halts as the arithmetic finishes.

"The banshee was your kin," she says softly. "A sister?"

"My sister," I say.

"You struck her down yourself?" Morbid fascination lights her eyes. "How did the transformation manifest? Did her flesh warp like the old chronicles claim? Did she retain any shred of her mortal mind before you ended it?"

I snap the notebook shut and look straight into her eyes. "If we are digging into agonizing memories, Melindra, I have a few questions of my own."

I drop my voice to a whisper, well below Daren's hearing range.

"Why did you lie about testing our capabilities during the ambush?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Literally, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Literally)


