choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Literally, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Literally)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Literally
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_saved == 0) {
"I have no idea what you mean," Melindra says, looking away. "I told no lies."

"Really?" I say. "You searched a week for champions to kill the King of Ollendor, and you planned to throw your one lead away because we struggled against a dragon squad?"

"Ollendor's defenses are brutal," Melindra says. "If you cannot break a few orcs, the royal guard will slaughter you."

"Then explain the stance," I say. "Bent knees, elbows clamped against your ribs. Novice elementalists use that posture to stabilize output when their mana channels are wild. Are you still struggling with elementary control after six centuries?"
}
#if(v_b2_ch8_saved == 1) {
"I have no idea what you mean," Melindra says, looking away. "I told no lies."

"Really?" I say. "You searched a week for champions to kill the King of Ollendor, and you planned to throw your one lead away because we struggled against a dragon squad?"

"Ollendor's defenses are brutal," Melindra says. "If you cannot break a few orcs, the royal guard will slaughter you."

"If you were testing us, why did you blast that orc off my back?" I ask.

"I acted on instinct!" Melindra snaps.

"And you used the novice stance," I say. "Bent knees, elbows clamped against your ribs. Novice elementalists use that posture to stabilize output when their mana channels are wild. Are you still struggling with elementary control after six centuries?"
}
"I—" Melindra starts, but I press the advantage.

"Arraka meant it literally when she said you have no restraint," I say. "Stillwater power comes with volatile output. A scholar like Eiden mastered the valves, but you never did. You cannot throttle your spells down. In a group melee, you risk vaporizing your allies—and breaking Daren's life-binding oath in the process."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Image, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Image)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Image
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_tornado == 0) {
"You are wrong," Melindra hisses. "I used that stance because I have not fought beside mortal allies in decades. I was shaking off rust."

"So rusty you reverted to apprentice drills?" I say. "It does not hold up. You struggle with fine output control."

Melindra glares at me in furious silence.

"Why conceal it?" I ask. "Is maintaining the myth of the flawless, all-powerful stillwater worth sabotaging our tactical coordination?"

"I swear to you," Melindra whispers, her eyes narrowing dangerously, "if you breathe a word of this to the others..."
}
#if(v_b2_ch8_tornado == 1) {
"You are wrong," Melindra hisses. "I used that stance because the cyclone drained my focus, and I was being cautious."

"Tiredness impairs stamina, not muscle memory," I say. "You struggle with fine output control."

Melindra glares at me in furious silence.

"Why conceal it?" I ask. "Is maintaining the myth of the flawless, all-powerful stillwater worth sabotaging our tactical coordination?"

"I swear to you," Melindra whispers, her eyes narrowing dangerously, "if you breathe a word of this to the others..."
}
"You will do what?" I ask. "Give me a stern look? The blood oath prevents you from raising a finger against us. You must be deeply regretting making that vow life-binding."

"A little," Melindra mutters.

"Relax," I tell her. "I have no reason to broadcast your flaws. If you prefer looking arrogant over admitting a weakness, that is your business."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Unlikeable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Unlikeable)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Unlikeable
TEXT:

"Oh?" Melindra's face transforms with a predatory, mischievous grin. "You find me unlikeable, do you?"

"Thoroughly unlikeable," I say.

"And you dislike my company?"

"I endure it."

"In that case, I have no choice but to torment you until you find me delightful," she says, beaming.

"That is not how affection works," I say.

"Of course it is," Melindra says, keeping stride with me. "We share half-lessathi blood. What possible reason could you have to reject me?"

"Your personality, for a start."

"Melindra," Daren calls as he finishes cinching his scabbard. "The river branches north. Which bank do we follow?"

"Follow the left bank," Melindra calls back. "We will catch up in a moment."

Daren hoists his pack and waves the others forward. As Kate passes, her eyes linger on us with sharp, cold suspicion.

"Regarding your lineage—" Melindra begins as I shoulder my pack.

I walk past her without answering, matching Daren's stride.

"I was speaking to you!" Melindra scurries to match my pace.

"Were you?" I say. "My apologies. I stopped listening."

"Apology accepted," Melindra says cheerfully. "As I was saying: one of your parents belonged to the lessathi inner council."

choice(""What makes you say that?"", B2-Ch08a-Civilized, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Civilized, v_b2_ch8_parents = 1)
choice(""You really weren't kidding about pestering me, were you?"", B2-Ch08a-Civilized, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Civilized, v_b2_ch8_parents = 0)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Civilized
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_parents == 0) {
"You really weren't kidding about pestering me, were you?"

"Do you want your family history or not?" Melindra asks.

"I have no interest in my biological parents," I say.

"Fine!" Melindra crosses her arms with a pout. "Stay ignorant."

"Gladly," I say.

We march in silence for twenty yards.

"So about that banshee..." Melindra begins.

"Are you serious?" I ask.

"I am curious!"

"Find a new hobby."

"If I had one, I would not be walking beside you, would I?"

"Quiet, both of you," Daren warns, his hand dropping to his hilt. "Multiple magical signatures closing fast from the scrub. On your guard."

Four figures step out from the brush ahead. They wear leather doublets set with glowing runic plates—weapon enchanters. They carry heavy blades, their bearing sloppy and arrogant.

"Look what wandered into our territory," their leader jeers, spitting into the grass. "These fools brought camp followers to a tournament."

"A girl for each of them," a second sneers.

"Did the dwarf claim the little one?" the third laughs. "Vile runt."

Melindra wraps both arms around my bicep, pressing herself tight against my side.

"Darling," she purrs loudly, batting her lashes with an exaggerated pout, "are you going to let these ruffians insult me? You will not allow them to walk away after calling me a whore, will you?"
}
#if(v_b2_ch8_parents == 1) {
"What makes you say that?"

"Intermarriage with humans carries severe penalties in lessathi law," Melindra says. "Covert affairs stay hidden, which is why half-lessathi are virtually nonexistent today. But a pregnancy cannot be concealed indefinitely. Your parents fled the continent because your mother's condition would have been discovered."

"Why assume high rank?" I ask.

"Only royal houses and lessathi leaders hold teleportation keys," Melindra says. "Leaving the continent required authorized access to a gateway relay. Your mother had allies at the top, or she held rank herself. Thirty years ago, the High Overseer was Heksol. Perhaps you are his kin. He died or was executed shortly after; I stopped following council politics."

"Quiet, both of you," Daren warns, his hand dropping to his hilt. "Multiple magical signatures closing fast from the scrub. On your guard."

Four figures step out from the brush ahead. They wear leather doublets set with glowing runic plates—weapon enchanters. They carry heavy blades, their bearing sloppy and arrogant.

"Look what wandered into our territory," their leader jeers, spitting into the grass. "These fools brought camp followers to a tournament."

"A girl for each of them," a second sneers.

"Did the dwarf claim the little one?" the third laughs. "Vile runt."

Melindra wraps both arms around my bicep, pressing herself tight against my side.

"Darling," she purrs loudly, batting her lashes with an exaggerated pout, "are you going to let these ruffians insult me? You will not allow them to walk away after calling me a whore, will you?"
}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Terrified, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Terrified)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Terrified
TEXT:

I turn my head. Kate is staring at Melindra with absolute, stunned disbelief.

Melindra catches the look and her smile sharpens. She pulls my arm closer, resting her cheek against my shoulder while maintaining dead-eyed eye contact with Kate. Kate's jaw clenches, and she turns her face away in cold disgust.

Melindra's grin widens.

"Do not worry, sweetheart," the lead enchanter snickers, resting his hand on his pommel. "We are not going anywhere until your gentlemen empty their packs of every pinecone they have gathered."

"Are you serious?" Daren lets out an incredulous bark of laughter.

"I see the scar failed to frighten them," Hadrik chuckles.

"They lack the wits to recognize it," Illuna says.

"We have no time for this," Kate says.

She drops her aura suppression. Frost erupts in a deafening crack across the turf, driving three serrated ice daggers deep into the dirt between the leader's boots.

"Banshee!" the man shrieks, stumbling backward into the riverbed. "It is an uncollared banshee! Run!"

He scrambles through the shallows, tearing into the opposing thicket with his companions sprinting behind him in blind panic.

"Can you release my arm now?" I ask Melindra.

"Certainly," Melindra says, letting go with a shrug. "It was merely a jest."

"Thrilling," I say. "Now take the lead and leave me in peace."

"Only if you walk beside me," Melindra purrs.

"Not a chance."

"Then I stay right here," she says.

"Barry," Hadrik rumbles with a wide grin, "did you negotiate a private alliance during lunch?"

"Be quiet," I mutter.

"Leave them be," Daren says, sighing. "She will tire of it eventually. Follow the bank."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Hobby, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Hobby)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Hobby
TEXT:

As Daren predicted, Melindra tires of trailing my heels after an hour and drifts forward to rejoin Flower, the only companion who tolerates her without friction. We march through the afternoon without incident, though we spot several contestant bands scouring the pines, stuffing pinecones into sacks with frantic energy.

At dusk, we pitch our camp on a dry bank overlooking the tributary. Melindra shows little interest in communal chores; the moment we halt, she wanders down to the water's edge, scouring the gravel for additions to her mineral collection.

With the canvas pitched and bedrolls unrolled, I sit against a fallen log and open my notebook to verify a lingering question on artifact theory. Flipping through my early entries, a margin note on lessathi energy converters catches my eye. According to the Western Continent scholars, a stat booster should siphon ambient mana from high-density spellcasting, yet neither my device nor Leila's logged a single point following yesterday's duel with the dragon.

I close the book and walk over to where Leila and Kate sit cleaning their gear.

"Leila," I say. "Have you a moment? I want to verify something regarding device calibration."

"Certainly," Leila writes in soft blue script.

"Since when do you study spell theory on the road?" Kate asks, looking up with an arched brow at the worn leather volume in my hands. "You never touched that ledger during our first weeks together."

"I took notes daily before crossing the sea," I say. "I shelved the habit when survival in Varathia required drawing steel every twenty minutes."

"What about the booster?" Leila writes.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Extract, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Extract)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Extract
TEXT:

"Did your device register an energy surge after the dragon engagement yesterday?" I ask.

"None," Leila writes. "Not a single allocation point."

"Precisely," I say. "The old texts claim the internal core draws from residual spell discharge. If true, a battle of that magnitude should have flooded the accumulator. Yet our devices recorded nothing. Have you observed that discrepancy before?"

"I have not tracked it closely," Leila writes, tilting her head.

"I have a hypothesis," I say. "When my hidden magical stats temporarily unlocked in the ruins, the surge burned through ambient mana instantly. If the device's intake buffer lacks the capacity to store rapid discharge, spell residue disperses into the ether before the core can convert it. Which leaves only one steady source."

"Mage auras," Leila writes. "Aura radiation is continuous and stable."

"Exactly," I say. "The intake pulls a fraction of a percent from every aura within its thirty-mile radius. Point generation depends on population density, not individual spell scale. It explains why our accumulation slowed as contestants were eliminated across the provinces—but it fails to explain why points stagnated while we were inside Thilias."

"Competition from other devices," Kate suggests, sharpening an ice dagger with a smooth whetstone. "You were hardly the only contestant who thought to purchase a lessathi booster from a vendor. If mortals across the continents bought them without realizing they require lessathi blood to operate, dozens of dormant units in the city were siphoning the same aura pool."

choice(""I guess you make a good point."", B2-Ch08a-Point, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Point)
choice(""Or maybe other mages have been staying away from Thilias for some reason..."", B2-Ch08a-Away, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Away)
choice(""Don't forget that there were also lessathi in the city."", B2-Ch08a-Lessathi, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Lessathi)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Point
TEXT:

"That is a reasonable assessment," I say.

"My sensor detects active boosters only within close proximity," Leila writes. "It logged no nearby signatures on the thoroughfares, but across a city of that scale, dozens could operate beyond our radius."

"On that subject," I say, looking at Leila, "you mentioned carrying level three strength and agility, yet your kinetic output in the arena matched an unboosted fighter. Is your core malfunctioning?"

"No," Leila writes. "My father explained that the device amplifies biological baseline. It multiplies existing muscle density and reflexive speed; it cannot manufacture mass. My constitution has always been frail, which caps the physical return at my current allocation."

"So my physical drills before the tournament were worthwhile after all," I say.

"You trained physically?" Kate looks up, skeptical. "This is the result? I could pin your arm to this log without using magic."

"I trained for six months while researching spell theory," I tell her. "I was preparing a foundation, not building a siege engine. Speaking of which—where is Melindra? She was scouring the riverbed five minutes ago."

"Who cares?" Kate says coldly.

"Her tent is not pitched," I say. "Is she sleeping under the stars?"

"She can sleep in the gravel for all I care," Kate says, sheathing her dagger. "She will not stand watch either way. I have the first rotation. Get into your bedrolls; neither of you is on the roster tonight. Take the rest."

We heed her advice and head for our canvas shelters. Inside my tent, I slide the notebook into my pack, unlace my boots, and settle onto the bedroll. The night is quiet, and sleep takes me quickly.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Away
TEXT:

"Or perhaps contestants avoided the capital entirely," I say.

"A distinct possibility," Kate says, testing the edge of her blade with a thumb. "With no clear instructions from the organizers, solitary mages would view fortified cities as chokepoints primed for ambushes."

"On that subject," I say, looking at Leila, "you mentioned carrying level three strength and agility, yet your kinetic output in the arena matched an unboosted fighter. Is your core malfunctioning?"

"No," Leila writes. "My father explained that the device amplifies biological baseline. It multiplies existing muscle density and reflexive speed; it cannot manufacture mass. My constitution has always been frail, which caps the physical return at my current allocation."

"So my physical drills before the tournament were worthwhile after all," I say.

"You trained physically?" Kate looks up, skeptical. "This is the result? I could pin your arm to this log without using magic."

"I trained for six months while researching spell theory," I tell her. "I was preparing a foundation, not building a siege engine. Speaking of which—where is Melindra? She was scouring the riverbed five minutes ago."

"Who cares?" Kate says coldly.

"Her tent is not pitched," I say. "Is she sleeping under the stars?"

"She can sleep in the gravel for all I care," Kate says, sheathing her dagger. "She will not stand watch either way. I have the first rotation. Get into your bedrolls; neither of you is on the roster tonight. Take the rest."

We heed her advice and head for our canvas shelters. Inside my tent, I slide the notebook into my pack, unlace my boots, and settle onto the bedroll. The night is quiet, and sleep takes me quickly.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Lessathi
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0) {
"Lessathi agents were operating inside the walls," I say.

"They carried no boosters," Leila writes. "The handler who confronted me in the holding cells possessed no hardware; my sensor remained dark."

"A lessathi threatened you?" Kate asks, her posture tightening.

"He demanded my device," Leila writes. "When I refused, he petitioned the arena master to confiscate it, but the master declined to preserve the spectacle for the stands."

"On that subject," I say, looking at Leila, "you mentioned carrying level three strength and agility, yet your kinetic output in the arena matched an unboosted fighter. Is your core malfunctioning?"

"No," Leila writes. "My father explained that the device amplifies biological baseline. It multiplies existing muscle density and reflexive speed; it cannot manufacture mass. My constitution has always been frail, which caps the physical return at my current allocation."
}
