"Who cares?" Kate says coldly.

"Her tent is not pitched," I say. "Is she sleeping under the stars?"

"She can sleep in the gravel for all I care," Kate says, sheathing her dagger. "She will not stand watch either way. I have the first rotation. Get into your bedrolls; neither of you is on the roster tonight. Take the rest."

We heed her advice and head for our canvas shelters. Inside my tent, I slide the notebook into my pack, unlace my boots, and settle onto the bedroll. The night is quiet, and sleep takes me quickly. 

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Unnoticed
TEXT:

Sleep claims me within minutes of resting my head on the canvas roll.

Barely an hour later, a soft, warm weight presses against my ribs.

I snap my eyes open in the pitch black. Melindra is wedging herself into the narrow clearance between my bedroll and the canvas wall, wriggling to fit her frame into the cramped shelter.

"What in the—" I start.

A slender hand claps over my mouth, her index finger pressing against my lips.

"Quiet," Melindra whispers, her breath warm against my ear. "You will alert the perimeter. Hold on—a sound barrier."

She murmurs a swift incantation under her breath. Instantly, the nocturnal chorus of crickets, the rustle of riverside reeds, and the distant rushing water vanish into absolute, deadened silence.

"Done," Melindra says at normal conversational volume, sitting back on her heels with a pleased look. "The barrier contains all acoustic vibrations inside the canvas. We can speak freely."

"What are you doing in my shelter?" I demand, pressing my back against the far canvas wall to claim whatever space remains. "Kate is patrolling the line. How did you slip past an elementalist on watch?"

"Simple," Melindra says. "A basic optical refraction spell with an aura damper. I unlatched the flap the moment she checked the eastern treeline."

"An aura-masking invisibility spell?" I ask.

"The one Eiden refined," she says. "He passed the matrix to me centuries ago."

"Right," I say, exhaling. "You bypassed our sentry. Now explain why you broke into my tent at midnight. What could possibly not wait until sunrise?"

"A minor oversight when packing," Melindra says smoothly. "When I unpacked my provisions this evening, I realized I left my canvas in Thilias."

"You forgot your tent?" I stare at her. "Why not ask Daren or Hadrik for a spare ground-sheet?"

"In case you failed to observe the march," Melindra says, "your companions are not exactly enamored with my presence."

"So you decided to invade my bedroll instead?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Insects, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Insects)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Insects
TEXT:

"I was not attempting to wake you!" Melindra says, throwing her hands up in exasperation. "Your tent is the size of a shipping crate! I intended to sleep until four in the morning and slip out before dawn. I tried resting on the riverbank, but the damp grass is crawling with beetles, and my passive sleep wards deflect curses, not wood mites!"

"Why my tent?" I ask. "Why not Flower's? Or Kate's?"

"Because sharing a blanket with Arraka's insults is torture," Melindra says flatly. "And your girlfriend would slit my throat before I cleared the threshold."

"Kate is not my girlfriend," I say.

"She certainly glared like one when I took your arm by the river," Melindra says.

"Kate glares at everyone," I say. "Except Leila."

"If you believe that girl feels nothing for you, you are remarkably blind."

"Listen," I say, keeping my tone level. "Kate spent ten years in the wilderness hunted as a monster. Her human contact consisted of bounty hunters with iron chains or terrified villagers running for their lives. She is not nursing romantic daydreams on a tournament trail. She came to find her friend; I came to win a prize that keeps me alive. Whatever dynamic you are imagining does not exist."

"Whatever helps you rest," Melindra says with an airy wave of her hand. "Speaking of which, I am exhausted. If you require anything, tap my shoulder. Goodnight."

She lays her head on the edge of the spare blanket and shuts her eyes.

"Melindra," I say, nudging her boot with mine. "You cannot sleep here."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Fair, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Fair)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Fair
TEXT:

"Why not?" Melindra asks without opening her eyes.

"Because this is my tent," I say.

"And it has ample room for two," she says, opening one eye to look at me. "Cramped, certainly, but manageable. You do not despise my presence so much that you cannot share four square yards of canvas."

"It is not about dislike," I say. "Sharing a single bedroll creates an awkward situation."

"Oh," Melindra says, sitting up and tilting her head. "I understand now. Fair enough. We are sharing quarters, after all."

"Exactly," I say. "So gather your boots and—wait, what are you doing?"

Melindra reaches for the collar of her silk blouse and begins undoing the fastenings.

"Taking off my clothes," Melindra says, pausing with her blouse half-loosened, looking genuinely puzzled. "Was that not your implication? That sharing a bed without intimacy is awkward?"

"No!" I say, waving my hands frantically. "I meant you should leave the tent!"

"Ah," Melindra says, raising an eyebrow with a slow, deliberate smirk. "So you are claiming you have no desire to sleep with me?"

choice(""I didn't say that!"", B2-Ch08a-Energizer, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Energizer, v_b2_ch8_energizer = 1)
choice(""Yes, that is exactly what I am saying."", B2-Ch08a-Uninterrupted, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Uninterrupted)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Uninterrupted
TEXT:

"Yes, that is exactly what I am saying."

"Your loss," Melindra says, rolling her eyes as she reties her collar. "In that case, I am going to sleep. See you at dawn."

"You are leaving this tent," I say. "Sleep with Flower, sleep in the grass, sleep in a tree. Out."

"You are genuinely throwing me out into the damp?" Melindra asks, her voice dropping the playful edge into something colder. "I asked for four hours of dry ground. I will not disturb you, and I will be gone before the first sentry change. It will be as if I were never here."

choice("Let her sleep in the tent.", B2-Ch08a-Sleepy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Sleepy, v_b2_ch8_energizer = 2)
choice("Tell her to get out of the tent.", B2-Ch08a-Regret, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Regret, v_b2_ch8_energizer = 0)
choice("Let her sleep in the tent for a few hours, and go join Kate on her night shift.", B2-Ch08a-Gentleman, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Gentleman, v_b2_ch8_energizer = 3)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Gentleman
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch8_gentleman,1)
achievement("A true gentleman",v_ac_b2_ch8_gentleman)
"Actually, I think I have another idea," I say.

I drop to my hands and knees, maneuvering carefully over her legs toward the shelter flap.

"What are you doing?" Melindra winces as my elbow grazes her chin. "Watch the elbows!"

"You take the tent for the night," I say, reaching for the toggle. "I will join Kate on her perimeter shift."

Melindra blinks at me in the dark. "Wait... what? You do not have to abandon your own shelter—"

"When Kate's shift ends," I say, "I will ask Daren or Hadrik to pitch an extra canvas for you before they rotate in. I will wake you when it is ready."

I draw the zipper back and slide out into the cool night air.

"Barry, listen—"

"Goodnight, Melindra," I say, pulling the canvas closed behind me.

choice("Next chapter", B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Energizer
TEXT:

"I didn't say that!"

"What did you say, then?" Melindra asks, her fingers lingering on her buttons.

"I was—are you actually serious right now? With you, it is impossible to separate the jokes from reality."

"Entirely serious," Melindra says. "Why wouldn't I be? I may carry the appearance of a nineteen-year-old girl, but I have six centuries of living behind me. I am well-acquainted with mortal men. I hold no aversion to you: we share half-lessathi heritage, you have a sharp mind, and your face is reasonably passable. Leaning slightly toward the rougher half, perhaps, but passable. You grasp the point."

"I cannot tell if that was a compliment or an insult," I say.

"The conclusion is simple," Melindra says, leaning closer. "Nothing bars us from enjoying ourselves for a few hours before morning. Stillwaters are sterile, so there is no concern about reproduction. The acoustic ward guarantees complete privacy for hours. The accommodations are cramped, but perfection is boring."

I look at her, then shake my head with a dry laugh. "Fair enough. Let us see where this goes."

"Much better," Melindra says, smiling brightly. "Before we begin, allow me to cast a vitality weave on you. Men have a habit of running out of breath after twenty minutes, and I dislike interruptions."

"As long as there are no lingering side effects," I say.

"None whatsoever," Melindra says breezily. "None that proved fatal, at least. I cast it on myself as well, so we share the matrix. Hold still."

She presses her palm against my sternum and whispers a rapid sequence of syllables.

A wave of intense, electric heat surges through my chest, radiating down my spine and into my limbs. Every muscle coils tight with boundless stamina. I feel as though I could sprint across a mountain range and still have breath to spare. The rush is incredible—though burning through mana reserves at this rate must carry a heavy physiological toll.

Still, a single evening will not break me.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Energizer2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Energizer2)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Energizer2
TEXT:

For the next several hours, the vitality weave carries us through the night. Fatigue simply refuses to settle. Despite the cramped canvas, shifting positions every half-hour as elbows knock against tent poles, neither of us calls for a halt.

When we finally untangle ourselves, the rush cools. We pull our clothes back on, and I prepare to grab whatever hour of true sleep remains before dawn.

"And you claimed you came to this tournament strictly for business," Melindra says with a smug grin, gathering her hair into her customary high ponytail.

"Fine," I say, buckling my belt. "A brief indulgence is not the end of the world."

"That is the spirit," Melindra says.

"We have perhaps an hour before Daren begins his morning inspection," I say, rolling onto my side. "Let us make the most of it."

"What are you talking about?" Melindra looks at me with genuine bewilderment. "It is broad daylight outside."

"What?" I look at the dark canvas ceiling. "Do not be ridiculous. The sun has not even crested the ridge. It is pitch black in here."

"The canvas is heavy and thick," Melindra says. "Check your chronometer. It has been morning for at least two hours."

Panic jolts through me. I yank the stat device from my belt pouch and illuminate the display.

The chronometer digits read well past eight in the morning.

I stare at the glowing numbers in disbelief. "How is this possible? It felt like two hours at most!"

"Vitality weaves distort temporal perception," Melindra says casually, fastening her cuffs. "Time slips away when the nervous system is running on mana. You will adapt eventually."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch08a-Energizer3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch08a-Energizer3)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Energizer3
TEXT:

"Why are you so nonchalant?" I demand, scrambling to lace my boots. "I got an hour of rest before midnight, but you stayed awake the entire night! How do you plan to march once the matrix expires?"

"Simple," Melindra says. "I recast the weave when we start dragging."

"Stacking continuous vitality weaves without rest strains the cardiovascular system," I say. "There are severe medical risks."

"A minor risk of heart failure," Melindra shrugs. "We are resilient. Well, you are moderately young, and I am immortal. We will manage."

"Right," I say, rubbing my temples. "Can you cast your invisibility weave so you can slip out of the tent unseen?"

"Ah," Melindra winces slightly. "Regarding that... Eiden's matrix was an incomplete transfer. It functions flawlessly, but it carries a strict twenty-four-hour cooldown. I exhausted today's charge when I slipped in last night."

"Splendid," I mutter, crawling toward the entrance flap. "No matter. The camp is dead quiet. Flower held the dawn shift; with luck, she is foraging by the water. I will check the clearing."

"Wait!" Melindra yells, lunging for my sleeve. "The sound ward is still active!"

I pull the zipper down and push the canvas flap wide.

The morning sunlight floods the interior.

Standing thirty feet away in a dead silence, Daren, Kate, Hadrik, Flower, and Leila are frozen mid-stride around the cold fire pit, every single pair of eyes locked squarely onto Melindra and me tumbling out of the tent.

"Right," I say.

choice("Next chapter", B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Sleepy
TEXT:

"All right, fine, you win!" I say. "But you'd better be out of here before the others wake up."

"Do not fret," Melindra says, tucking her legs beneath the spare blanket. "I am an early riser. I will be gone before the birds stir."

"Good," I say. "Sleep. Tomorrow is a hard march."

We settle into our respective corners of the canvas, and exhaustion pulls me under within minutes.

When my eyes open, the light filtering through the weave of the canvas feels far too bright.

I roll over. Melindra is fast asleep, curled in a tight ball, breathing softly.

"Melindra!" I shake her shoulder hard. "Wake up! Look at the light!"

I shove the glowing display of my stat booster directly in front of her half-open eyes.

"It is past dawn!" I whisper frantically. "Wake up and cast that invisibility spell so you can clear the campsite like you promised!"

"Cannot cast it..." Melindra mumbles, swatting lazily at the device. "Once a day... already used last night..."

"Magnificent," I mutter, scrambling toward the tent opening on hands and knees. "The clearing sounds quiet. Flower had the final rotation. I will inspect the perimeter, and on my mark, you sprint for the riverbank. Understood?"

"Mmhmm..." Melindra groans, propping herself up on one elbow.

"Brace yourself," I say, grasping the zipper pull.

"Wait!" Melindra gasps, her eyes snapping wide as she remembers. "The sound ward is still active!"

I rip the zipper down and thrust my head out into the morning air.

Thirty paces away, Daren, Kate, Hadrik, Flower, and Leila stand gathered around the dead embers of the fire pit, frozen mid-motion, their collective gaze fixed squarely on my unzipped tent and the stillwater girl scrambling behind me.

"Right," I say.

choice("Next chapter", B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: B2-Ch08a-Regret
TEXT:

"I already told you," I say. "I do not want you sleeping in my tent. Go find shelter elsewhere."

Melindra's expression hardens into something unusually sharp and cold. "If you are determined to drive me out into the damp, so be it. But remember this moment, mortal. You will regret it."

She reaches past me, pulls the brass zipper down, and pushes through the flap into the night.

"Wait!" I call out in a harsh whisper. "You forgot to mask your presence!"

Melindra ignores me, letting the canvas flap drop shut behind her.

I sit in the dark, listening to the silence of the campsite.

That could have gone smoother. With luck, Kate was patrolling the northern tree line and missed her exit. Then again, why should I fret? If Kate noticed, the truth is straightforward enough to explain. And Melindra's parting threat carries no weight; Daren's life-binding oath prevents her from lifting a finger against us.

There is nothing she can do to harm me. Right?

choice("Next chapter", B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


